9. Old Norse — doctrine 53: the ǫ/ø -> ö merger, per RELATION
  PASS  the module recognises the merged orthography from the text
  PASS  aðalhending on two merged vowels is None, NOT True
          returning True would assert an identity the edition destroyed
  PASS  spelled ǫ : ǫ it resolves to True
  PASS  spelled ǫ : ø it resolves to False
          'this edition cannot tell me' and 'these vowels differ' are different answers
  PASS  skothending across the merger stays definite: jörð : fyrðum
          no reading of ö is y, so the merger cannot touch this verdict
  PASS  and its aðalhending verdict is definite too, and False
  PASS  skothending on two identical merged graphemes is None
          the merger is harmless for skothending, not weightless
  PASS  æ is exact by default
  PASS  æ is ambiguous once the merged orthography is declared
          classically bæði (æ) and grœði (œ) are different vowels, so the merged text would manufacture this aðalhending
  PASS  spelled œ it resolves to False

9b. Old Norse — when BOTH onsets are empty, the vowel is the initial letter
  PASS  Iðrask:yðrum is a skothending, not an onset collision
  PASS  ár:aura is a skothending, not an onset collision
  PASS  orms:armi is a skothending, not an onset collision
  PASS  endr:undir is a skothending, not an onset collision
  PASS  ungr:Engla is a skothending, not an onset collision
  PASS  same vowel and no onset either: nothing distinguishes them
          an aðalhending needs the initial letters to DISTINGUISH the words, and two vowel-initial words with the same vowel do not
  PASS  same MERGED grapheme and no onset: refuses, never decides
          ö : ö cannot say whether the vowels are the same OR different, so neither predicate may answer
  PASS  but a merged grapheme against a disjoint one stays DEFINITE
          ö reads {ǫ,ø} and i reads {i}; the sets are disjoint, so the vowels differ whichever way the merger resolves — attested

10. Old Norse — the vowel-length position, taken and declared
  PASS  a : á is NOT an aðalhending — length is a phoneme
  PASS  a : á IS a skothending — two different vowels is what it asks for
          the alternative reading, that a bare quantity contrast is too weak to be heard as a hending, is defensible and is NOT taken; it is declared in skothending.__doc__
  PASS  the position is stated in the docstring, not left to the reader
  PASS  and the docstring's own example obeys the onset rule
          same onset f-, so it is not a hending at all — Snorri: 'upphafstafir greina orðin'
  PASS  a long vowel and a diphthong are both heavy

11. Old Norse — doctrine 35: prominence is stress, weight is not
  PASS  stress is fixed on the first syllable of the word
  PASS  prominences() returns a real stress pattern (Old Norse HAS stress)
  PASS  sonar: stressed-LIGHT then unstressed-HEAVY
          collapsing weight into prominence would erase exactly this
  PASS  a closed syllable is heavy even with a short vowel
  PASS  a hyphen marks a compound, and its second element is stressed
          unhyphenated, the module has no morphology and says so

